{
 "id": "SHW-16",
 "name": "solar-system",
 "version": 1,
 "title": "A 3D solar system that runs on the clock",
 "summary": "The Sun and eight planets in 3D on a twinkling starfield: the Sun is a point light, so every planet shows a lit day side; the planets start where they really are today (mean longitudes from J2000 and the date) and run faster than real time at a chosen speed (a day, a week, a month or a year a second). A drag tilts and turns the camera from edge-on to straight down; tapping a planet's chip highlights its orbit and rings and names it in 3D; the panel fills a phone's width and sits in the corner of a wide window. Each orbit lies in its real plane (J2000 inclination and ascending node), with a Tilt ×5 checkbox to make the planes visibly different; a Zoom checkbox eases the camera in on the selected planet and follows it, gliding to a newly chosen one.",
 "prompt": "Make a 3D solar system: the Sun and planets on their orbits moving with the clock but sped up, with a speed control, orbit rings, a starry background, drag to tilt the view, and tap a planet's name to see where it is.",
 "level": 5,
 "family": "SHW",
 "hand_built": "Scene3D has no clock-driven orbits, point light or projected labels: the camera orbit, the sun-as-light, the date-anchored simulated time and the 2D highlight projected from 3D are written by hand.",
